icity.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Serious adverse effects include hepatorenal injury and enhanced immunosuppression\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tacrolimus is directly linked to hepatorenal damage, particularly in renal transplant patients whose kidney function is already compromised. One case even reported fatal cardiac arrest due to hyperkalemia induced by tacrolimus-related aldosterone suppression [Data: Relationships (8593)]. Additionally, tacrolimus can cause excessive immunosuppression, increasing susceptibility to infections and other complications [Data: Relationships (8551)]. Cyclosporine, when subtherapeutic due to drug interactions (e.g., with St. John’s Wort), can lead to acute graft rejection, as evidenced by biopsy-confirmed cases [Data: Relationships (13949)]. These adverse outcomes underscore the high-stakes nature of managing these agents.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Neurological risks extend beyond immunosuppressants to interacting drugs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While cyclosporine and tacrolimus themselves are not directly tied to seizure activity in the provided data, carbamazepine—a drug that interacts with this network by inducing enzymes that lower cyclosporine levels—is associated with epilepsy-like seizures, especially when combined with monoamine oxidase inhibitors [Data: Relationships (9696)]. This highlights that the risk profile of the community extends beyond the primary immunosuppressants to include interacting medications that introduce additional neurological hazards. Such cross-cutting risks complicate polypharmacy management in transplant or chronically ill patients.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Classification consistency reveals tacrolimus as a multifaceted CNI agent\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tacrolimus is consistently classified across multiple synonymous drug class labels—including CNI, calcineurin inhibitor, and calmodulin-depende